// "3. Unique & Off-the-Beaten-Path" — có mô tả đầy đủ.
const offBeatenPath = [
  { name: 'Nậm Cang', note: "A small Red Dao community about 30km from Sa Pa town, down a road rough enough that most tour buses turn back. Terraced valleys unfold slower here, without the souvenir-stall crowding of Sa Pa's main trails. Trek between rice paddies still worked by hand, stay the night in a homestay with a family Morning Vietnam has worked with for years, and wake to mist still sitting in the valley below." },
  { name: 'Lai Châu', note: 'Famous for its breathtaking sea of clouds, where mountain peaks rise above a blanket of white mist at sunrise. Combined with its stunning highland scenery, it is a perfect destination for nature lovers, photographers, and anyone seeking unforgettable mountain views.' },
  { name: 'Mai Châu', note: 'A quiet postcard unfolds between the hills, just a couple of hours from Hanoi. Wooden stilt houses rise above a patchwork of rice paddies, framed by soft blue mountains in the late afternoon light. Cycle through White Thai villages, settle into a homestay for the night, or simply watch farmers at work in the fields below.' },
  { name: 'Y Tý', note: 'A cloud-forest village near the Chinese border where sea-of-cloud mornings roll through the valley before burning off by mid-morning. Hà Nhì communities here still build earthen "mushroom houses" with thick clay walls and thatched roofs, built to hold warmth through the mountain winters. Chase the cloud sea at sunrise from Lảo Thẩn peak, wander between hamlets on foot, or visit during the Gạ Ma Do festival if your timing lines up.' },
  { name: 'Pù Luông', note: "Layered rice terraces roll across the valley floor here, framed by the Pù Luông Nature Reserve's forested peaks, with none of Sa Pa's crowds. Thai stilt houses dot the hillsides, water wheels turn slowly along the streams, and the pace of the whole valley seems to slow to match them. Trek between villages on foot or by bicycle, cool off at Hieu Waterfall, or watch the terraces flood and mirror the sky just before planting season." },
  { name: 'Mù Cang Chải', note: 'Genuine family homestays here with only the basics are part of what keeps this valley feeling undiscovered despite its rice terraces being among the most photographed in the country. La Pán Tẩn, Chế Cu Nha and Dế Xu Phình offer three distinct terrace formations within a short drive of each other. Trek between viewpoints during the September–October harvest, join a local family in the fields, or simply watch the whole valley turn gold at sunset.' },
  { name: 'Hoàng Su Phì (Hà Giang)', note: "Rice terraces here don't just cascade down one hillside, they wrap entire mountain ranges in overlapping curves, among the most dramatic in Vietnam and far less photographed than Sa Pa's. La Chí, Dao and Nùng communities have carved these fields by hand for generations." },
  { name: 'Măng Đen', note: 'A hill town in the Central Highlands often called "a second Đà Lạt," minus the crowds. Pine forests wrap around a scatter of lakes and waterfalls, and the air stays noticeably cooler than the lowlands just below. Walk among the pines at Đắk Ke Lake, visit a Ba Na or Xơ Đăng village nearby, or simply enjoy a town that still feels like it hasn\'t been discovered yet.' },
  { name: 'Mũi Né', note: "A coastal town shaped by wind, sand, and endless dunes. Towering red and white sand dunes roll along the coast, drawing dune-buggy rides and quiet sunrise walks. Strong ocean winds make it one of Southeast Asia's top spots for kitesurfing, while nearby fishing villages keep the pace refreshingly slow." },
  { name: 'Côn Đảo Island', note: "Ha Long Bay's calmer twin, rising from the same emerald water with a fraction of the boat traffic. The terrains here are just as dramatic, but the coves stay quiet enough to hear your own paddle. Kayak between limestone islands, swim off a beach with no one else on it, or stay overnight on the water for a sunrise no day-tripper gets to see." },
  { name: 'Cái Răng Floating Market', note: "Before sunrise, boats loaded with pineapples, watermelons and dragon fruit begin gathering on the water outside Cần Thơ, each one hanging a sample of its cargo from a tall pole so buyers can spot it from a distance. This is the Mekong Delta's largest floating market, and it's still very much a working one — arrive by 6am to catch it at its busiest, before the tour boats do. Drift between vendor boats by smaller sampan, buy breakfast straight off the water, or watch the market wind down as the delta's ordinary day begins around it." },
  { name: 'Trà Sư Cajuput Forest', note: 'A vast flooded cajuput forest in the Mekong Delta, where emerald-green duckweed blankets the waterways and birds glide quietly overhead. Wooden boats drift through narrow channels beneath towering trees, revealing one of southern Vietnam\'s richest wetland ecosystems.' },
];

// Mục riêng của Canva, không đánh số — chèn liền sau "Off-the-Beaten-Path".
const trekkingParks = [
  { name: 'Trekking in Tà Chì Nhù', note: "One of Vietnam's most popular trekking peaks, rising 2,979 meters above sea level in Yên Bái Province. Renowned for its breathtaking sea of clouds and panoramic mountain views, the summit offers unforgettable sunrises above a blanket of mist. In autumn, its hills are covered with golden wild grass, creating one of the most iconic landscapes in northern Vietnam." },
  { name: 'Trekking in Pu Ta Leng', note: "Vietnam's second-highest mountain, standing at 3,049 meters above sea level. Known for its challenging trekking routes, ancient forests, and spectacular sea of clouds, it offers one of the most rewarding hiking experiences in northern Vietnam. During spring, the mountain comes alive with vibrant rhododendron blooms." },
  { name: 'Cúc Phương National Park', note: "Vietnam's first and oldest national park, where thousand-year-old trees rise out of dense limestone-forest canopy. Ancient cave systems, resident primates and one of the country's most respected endangered-species rescue centers all sit within a short walk of each other. Trek to the Cave of Prehistoric Man, cycle the park's quiet roads at dawn when wildlife is most active, or visit the Primate Rescue Center." },
];
